/** @namespace nCine::RHI
	@brief Render Hardware Interface — compile-time-selectable graphics backend abstraction

	Exactly one backend is compiled into a binary, selected by a `WITH_RHI_*` macro (the OpenGL
	family is the default). The render pipeline refers only to the backend-neutral aliases declared
	here (e.g. @ref nCine::RHI::Device, @ref nCine::RHI::Texture), so each backend only has to provide
	the same set of names with the same surface.
*/
/** @file nCine/Graphics/RHI/Rhi.h
	@brief Definitions of the classes aliased in @ref nCine::RHI for the selected backend
*/
/** @file nCine/Graphics/RHI/RhiFwd.h
	@brief Compile-time backend selection, forward declarations and the backend-neutral aliases of @ref nCine::RHI
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/GL
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::GL
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GL
	@brief OpenGL 3.3 core, OpenGL|ES 3.0/2.0 and WebGL 2.0 backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_GL`
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/Software
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::Software
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Software
	@brief CPU software rasterizer backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_SOFTWARE`
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Software::sw
	@brief Runtime support types for the transpiled (GLSL→C++) software shaders
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Software::sw::detail
	@brief Implementation details of the software shader runtime
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Software::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/D3D11
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::D3D11
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::D3D11
	@brief Direct3D 11 backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_D3D11`
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::D3D11::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/Vulkan
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::Vulkan
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Vulkan
	@brief Vulkan backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_VULKAN`
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::Vulkan::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/GX
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::GX
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GX
	@brief Nintendo Wii and GameCube fixed-function GX backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_GX`

	Has off-screen render targets but no programmable shaders, so every effect is submitted as a list of
	@ref nCine::RHI::FixedFunctionPass that the device turns into a TEV and blending setup.
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GX::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/PVR
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::PVR
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::PVR
	@brief Sega Dreamcast fixed-function PowerVR backend on top of KallistiOS, selected by `WITH_RHI_PVR`

	Like @ref nCine::RHI::GX it has render targets but no programmable shaders and consumes
	@ref nCine::RHI::FixedFunctionPass, here translated into tile accelerator polygon headers.
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::PVR::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/GU
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::GU
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GU
	@brief PlayStation Portable fixed-function GU backend, selected by `WITH_RHI_GU`

	Drives the Graphics Engine through `sceGu`, again with render targets, no programmable shaders and
	effects described by @ref nCine::RHI::FixedFunctionPass.
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GU::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/
/** @dir nCine/Graphics/RHI/GXM
	@brief Namespace @ref nCine::RHI::GXM
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GXM
	@brief PlayStation Vita backend driving `sceGxm` natively, selected by `WITH_RHI_GXM`

	Unlike the fixed-function console backends this is a full-pipeline backend, because the console's
	PowerVR SGX is a unified-shader part --- compared to the OpenGL path on the same console it only
	removes vitaGL, the OpenGL|ES 2.0 layer that would otherwise sit in between.
*/
/** @namespace nCine::RHI::GXM::UniformTypeInfo
	@brief Free-function helpers that classify reflected uniform types (component count, float-ness)
*/

